# VirtualMeet Client Environment Variables.
# Copy to client/.env.local (gitignored) and fill in. Vite only exposes vars
# prefixed VITE_, and it INLINES them into the built bundle — never put
# anything here that must stay secret from the browser.

# ── Server ────────────────────────────────────
# Where the Socket.IO/REST server lives. Leave unset for local and LAN play:
# the client then derives it from the page's own hostname (see
# src/services/serverUrl.ts). Set it when the server is somewhere else —
# behind a tunnel, or a real deployment.
# VITE_SERVER_URL="https://xxxx.trycloudflare.com"

# ── TURN (WebRTC relay) ───────────────────────
# Optional. Unset = STUN only, which is fine on a shared LAN but fails on
# office/campus WiFi and symmetric-NAT mobile networks: the peer connects,
# chat and movement work, and the camera just silently never appears.
# TURN relays the media when a direct path is impossible.
#
# All THREE must be set together — a TURN entry without credentials is
# rejected by the browser. With any of them missing the app falls back to
# STUN only, so a half-filled config degrades cleanly instead of breaking.
#
# VITE_TURN_URL accepts several comma-separated URLs; listing both the UDP
# and the TCP/TLS :443 endpoint matters, because the TCP one is what gets
# through firewalls that drop UDP outright.
#
# Get free credentials at https://www.metered.ca/tools/openrelay/ (free tier
# is ~20 GB relay/month — enough to prove it works, not enough for daily
# office use; production needs a paid TURN service).
#
# NOTE: these end up readable in the browser. That is inherent to browser
# TURN, not a mistake here — keeping them in env means they can be rotated
# or revoked without a code change, and never enter git.
# VITE_TURN_URL="turn:xxx.metered.live:443,turns:xxx.metered.live:443?transport=tcp"
# VITE_TURN_USERNAME=""
# VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=""
